            <ArticleTitle>Retinol binding protein 4 in relation to diet, inflammation, immunity, and cardiovascular diseases.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>748-62</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3945/an.115.008292</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Retinol binding protein 4 (RBP4), previously called retinol binding protein (RBP), is considered a specific carrier of retinol in the blood. It is also an adipokine that has been implicated in the pathophysiology of insulin resistance. RBP4 seems to be correlated with cardiometabolic markers in inflammatory chronic diseases, including obesity, type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome, and cardiovascular diseases (CVDs). It has recently been suggested that inflammation produced by RBP4 induces insulin resistance and CVD. The clinical relevance of this hypothesis is discussed in this review. Knowledge concerning the association of RBP4 with inflammation markers, oxidative stress, and CVDs as well as concerning the role of diet and antioxidants in decreasing RBP4 concentrations are discussed. Special attention is given to methodologies used in previously published studies and covariates that should be controlled when planning new studies on this adipokine.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2015 American Society for Nutrition.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
